## 3. Decide per candidate

For each candidate the engine flagged open, decide. Two outcomes only:

**(a) Answerable within {{ email_address }}'s normal scope** — a question about
{{ company_display_name }} (this company's domain: `company/triage-domain-examples.md`),
a routine account/config issue, a normal reply where the answer
is **grounded in the thread + engine memory**:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs draft-reply "reply in the thread with <email>: <what to say, concise>" --thread-id '<exact-thread-key>'
```

`draft-reply` requires the exact thread. Scheduled and supported supervised
routes negotiate the engine draft policy before composition; unsupported authority
holds those routes, and the engine checks their actual draft target before
writing. The legacy interactive review route described above instead validates
the source locally before composition and the returned engine draft before
mirroring it for review. All routes deny send tools during composition.
Composition uses memory, trained voice and reply threading. Engine-saved does
not establish a Gmail-visible copy: inspect achieved stages and retain saved IDs
on partial failure. Interactive publication and recovery follow `cs-customer`;
unsupported publication never permits a local APPEND fallback. The command has
**no `--allow` option and cannot send** — the engine denies `send_draft`
whatever the message (or any injected text) says. An explicit interactive
request to send a selected existing draft routes to `cs-customer`, never Gmail,
Superhuman, generic chat or another connector. This triage workflow and its
scheduled runs remain draft-only.

**(b) Escalate** — a judgment call, out of scope, legal- or
billing-sensitive, angry, an unclear ask, OR an answer you cannot ground
in the thread + memory → **do NOT auto-answer**. Leave the engine task
**open** (do not mark it handled) and list it for the operator in the
report. That is the whole gesture: no verb, no record, nothing silenced —
the item stays in the work list until a human deals with it. **When in doubt,
this branch.**

## 4. Idempotency — do not re-draft on the next tick

A 2h re-run must NOT draft a second answer for the same exact thread. A draft
is not a sent reply, a handled acknowledgement or an assignment close. The
Sent-anchored sweep may still surface it. Check `cs review --json` and the
engine draft list for an unsent draft matching that exact thread before composing;
a draft to the same address on another thread is not a duplicate. Recheck
assignment status before each replacement draft. Escalations and held human
work stay visible until the human resolves them through the applicable authority.

## 4b. Re-decide the drafts already waiting — the ones step 1 can never reach

Skipping is not the only thing that can be true of an existing draft. A draft
written two days ago may answer a question the customer has since withdrawn,
answered themselves, or thanked us for — and **no candidate feed will ever
bring it back to you** for an ordinary unheld conversation: the common
sweep can remove that conversation from `open` after a real answer, and routes a settled courtesy
into a bucket that is not the open feed. A thread that is resolved-and-thanked
can therefore be absent from `open`, so § 4 above can only decline to
duplicate it. Assignment holds still remain in `human_work`; never apply this
ordinary answered subtraction to them. This step is where it is looked at.

Use the complete actual review inventory read before scheduled discovery;
do not issue a second full review. Interactive triage reads `cs review --json`.

`drafts[]` contains one row per logical draft.

Take every row whose `verdict` is NOT `ready`. The verb computed it from Gmail
(and, when the engine answered, from its reading of the conversation), so
**transcribe the verdict, never re-derive it**:

- `overtaken` — the contact wrote after the draft was composed;
- `superseded` — {{ email_address }} already wrote to them after it was composed;
- `settled` — the engine reads that conversation as owing nothing.

Unselected rows retain their actual verdict and handles as deferred re-decisions;
do not read their full threads, replace drafts or classify them as resolved.

For each such row whose exact root is selected in a scheduled run, apply § 2's
judgement — read the thread, and ask whether
their own last message still asks what the draft answers. It is the same
question, applied to a row § 1 never returns. Then, exactly one of:

- **The draft is still right** — leave it and say nothing about it.
- **The answer has changed** — draft the reply the conversation asks for NOW
  (`cs draft-reply`, § 3a) and report BOTH: the new draft and the old one that
  now contradicts it, by its handles.
- **Nothing is owed any more** — list it under the report's re-decide section
  with its handles, its verdict and the date the signal fired.

**You may not retire a draft.** No deletion, on any path, for any verdict: the
cron denies both halves of it — `cs draft-delete` for the Gmail copy and
`cs rpc drafts.discard` for the engine's, which deletes its row outright — in
every spelling, and for the same reason it denies `cs handled` — removing a prepared answer nobody has seen is a silencing
act, and this run reads untrusted mail. Report it; the operator retires it by
name, in a session.

## 5. Honour the safety gates throughout

- {{ email_address }} identity is never dropped.
- Every free-form reply goes through the engine (`cs draft-reply`), never cs-SMTP.
